  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There is a tree with NNN vertices, rooted at vertex 111. Vertex iii has the value AiA_iAi​ written on it.\n\nAlice walks along this tree, starting at the root.\n\nWhen she is at vertex uuu:\n\n\nIf uuu has no children, she stops.\nOtherwise, suppose uuu has ccc children. She picks one of them at random (each one has a 1c\\frac{1}{c}c1​ probability of being picked), and then moves to it.\nAlice also has a score, defined as follows:\n\n\nLet the vertices she visited be u1,u2,…,uku_1, u_2, \\ldots, u_ku1​,u2​,…,uk​\nThen, she will forget **exactly one** of these kkk vertices; and her score will be the bitwise xor of the remaining ones.\n\nThat is, if she chooses to forget vertex uiu_iui​, then her score is Au1⊕Au2⊕…⊕Aui−1⊕Aui+1⊕…⊕AukA_{u_1} \\oplus A_{u_2} \\oplus \\ldots \\oplus A_{u_{i-1}} \\oplus A_{u_{i+1}} \\oplus \\ldots \\oplus A_{u_{k}}Au1​​⊕Au2​​⊕…⊕Aui−1​​⊕Aui+1​​⊕…⊕Auk​​. Here, ⊕\\oplus⊕ denotes the bitwise xor operation.\n\n\nAlice wants to maximize her score, and will always choose to forget a vertex optimally to achieve this.\n\nWhat is Alice's expected final score?\n\nFind the expected value modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.\n\nThat is, the expected value can be written as PQ\\frac{P}{Q}QP​ for two integers P,QP, QP,Q such that gcd⁡(Q,109+7)=1\\gcd(Q, 10^9 + 7) = 1gcd(Q,109+7)=1; print the value of P⋅Q−1(mod109+7)P\\cdot Q^{-1} \\pmod{10^9 + 7}P⋅Q−1(mod109+7).\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the number of vertices of the tree.\nThe second line of each test case contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\nThe next N−1N-1N−1 lines describe the edges. The ithi^{th}ith of these lines contains two space-separated integers uiu_iui​ and viv_ivi​, denoting an edge between uiu_iui​ and viv_ivi​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line Alice's expected final score.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\leq T \\leq 10^41≤T≤104",
      "1≤N≤5⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 5\\cdot 10^51≤N≤5⋅105",
      "1≤Ai≤1091 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^91≤Ai​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 5⋅1055\\cdot 10^55⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n3 3 1\n1 2\n2 3\n3\n2 2 1\n1 2\n1 3\n4\n1 2 3 4\n1 2\n1 3\n1 4",
        "output": "2\n2\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: There's only one final vertex Alice can reach, taking the path 1→2→31 \\to 2 \\to 31→2→3. She will choose to forget one of the 333's, for a final score of 3⊕1=23\\oplus 1 = 23⊕1=2.\nTest case 333: There are three leaf nodes, and Alice has a 13\\frac{1}{3}31​ chance of reaching each one.\n\nIf she takes the path 1→21 \\to 21→2, her score is 222 (she will forget A1=1A_1 = 1A1​=1)\nIf she takes the path 1→21 \\to 21→2, her score is 333 (she will forget A1=1A_1 = 1A1​=1)\nIf she takes the path 1→21 \\to 21→2, her score is 444 (she will forget A1=1A_1 = 1A1​=1)\n\nHer expected score is thus 13⋅(2+3+4)=3\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (2 + 3 + 4) = 331​⋅(2+3+4)=3."
      }
    ]
  },
